+   summarise(mean_yield_t_ha = mean(yield_t_ha, na.rm = TRUE),
+             n_fields = n()) |&gt;
+   arrange(desc(mean_yield_t_ha))
&gt; variety_summary_100
# A tibble: 3 × 3
  variety    mean_yield_t_ha n_fields
  &lt;chr&gt;                &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;int&gt;
1 InVigor               3.10       38
2 Clearfield            3.06       34
3 DEKALB                2.98       34



Do not read it as one giant command; read it one step at a time:

Filter to the fields that received at least 100 kg/ha.
Create the variable the question needs: metric yield.
Group the observations by variety.
Summarise each group down to one row: its mean yield and its number of fields.
Sort from highest mean yield to lowest.

Each line does one simple thing, and that is the reason for pipes: a good pipeline makes the logic of an analysis visible. Saving the table is one more line: write_csv(variety_summary_100, "output/variety_summary_100.csv").

the data: a data frame with the data to be plotted;
the mapping: aes(), which assigns variables to visual properties – horizontal position, vertical position, colour;
a geom: the mark to draw – geom_col() for bars, geom_line() for lines, geom_point() for points.


data |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = ..., y = ...)) +
  geom_something() + ...

18.7 Cutting a raster with a polygon
crop() trims a raster to a polygon’s bounding box and mask() blanks the cells outside the polygon itself. Together they cut one RM out of the slice, and freq() counts what is in it:


# Wood Creek, in the raster CRS
wood_creek &lt;- rms_utm |&gt;
  filter(RMNO == "281")

# Trim to the bounding box, then blank the cells outside the RM
aci_wc &lt;- crop(aci, vect(wood_creek)) |&gt;
  mask(vect(wood_creek))

# Count the cells in each class and turn counts into shares
freq(aci_wc) |&gt;
  left_join(classes, by = "value") |&gt;       # attach the crop names
  mutate(share = count / sum(count)) |&gt;     # share of the RM
  select(crop, count, share) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(share)) |&gt;
  head(6)

